1
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,800
Writing is only part of the annual process.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,500
Once the OP1 document has been completed,

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,000
the readers, typically senior leaders, now assume the primary role.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,800
Their job is to critically read the plan, ask probing questions,

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,300
provide constructive feedback, discuss and debate controversial topics,

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,000
and ultimately make company-wide decisions on which initiatives to greenlight

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
at a given funding level.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,500
As a senior leader, reading an OP1 document,

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:35,300
ask yourself these questions while reading,

10
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:39,000
and enter your answers in the document directly,

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
or ask them once the discussion begins.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The first question is, do I clearly understand

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
how this team will create additional customer value next year?

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Am I looking at the right input and output metrics?

15
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,000
If not, which ones are missing or incorrect?

16
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,500
Is the detailed wording of each metric exactly what we want?

17
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:04,000
Do any of these initiatives or goals merit dedicated time

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
from the executive team to review throughout the year?

19
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
If so, these will become S-team goals, which we will cover in the next lesson.

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Does the team have too many or too few resources

21
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,000
to accomplish each of the work streams and initiatives outlined in the plan?

22
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,300
Is the team thinking big enough?

23
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,000
Do they have the right mix of activities, including bold bets,

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
improving the core business, and fixing paper cuts?

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,300
Where are the risks with this plan?

26
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:37,500
Have they been identified and properly mitigated?

27
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,300
Are there any dependencies on my organization?

28
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,000
If so, has someone from my organization reviewed them,

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,000
and are they accurate?

30
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
For each initiative, are all of the dependencies called out and resolved?

31
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Are these the most important initiatives that will move the needle

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
in a meaningful way for this business?

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Are the team's key assumptions true?

34
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,300
A good technique to answer this question is to look at the inverse case.

35
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:07,000
Try to find any case where the assumption is not true.

36
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,700
And finally, you do not want to leave the room

37
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:14,000
until the answer to the following question is yes.

38
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,700
Do I believe that these initiatives and input goals

39
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,700
can be met with the requested investment of resources,

40
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,700
and if they are met, will we hit our output goals?

41
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:28,300
As you ask questions, be direct and constructive.

42
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:31,500
Ask questions that will drive deeper thinking.

43
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:34,800
The ultimate goal is to leave the room with a solid plan.

44
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,300
The Presenters:

45
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:41,300
At minimum, the presenters, who are the team that wrote the document,

46
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:46,200
needs to include the business unit leader, key team members,

47
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:49,000
one of whom is the designated note taker,

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,200
the finance counterpart, and representatives from any other team

49
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:57,000
that have big dependencies that will invest significant resources

50
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
in any part of the proposed initiatives.

51
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
And lastly, the HR lead for the group.

52
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,700
Make sure you have access to any supplemental data or documents,

53
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:09,500
not in the OP1 plan, that could be helpful.

54
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,000
Examples of this include last year's OP1 plan,

55
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
your latest QBR or MBR,

56
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
your latest weekly business review or WBR,

57
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,500
detailed customer demographic and usage data and so forth.

58
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:26,000
The note taker cannot be the primary person who answers questions

59
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
in a meeting, and they need to be a team member who is deeply

60
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,700
familiar with the details of the business, not an admin.

61
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:37,000
Make sure to answer all questions concisely and with data.

62
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,700
If you don't know the answer, and no one else from your team does either,

63
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:44,000
then say you don't know and I will get back to you.

64
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,500
This last tip is easier said than done, but is largely under your control.

65
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,000
Oh, and finally, make sure you get a good night's sleep

66
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,500
the night before the meeting.

67
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,000
The meeting should last 90 to 120 minutes.

68
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
If your business area is complex or large,

69
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and you can't fit at all into that time,

70
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
make sure you create separate documents for those parts of your business

71
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,500
and allocate separate meeting slots for them.

72
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:11,500
In most cases, for an Amazon meeting,

73
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:15,000
the document is six pages long plus appendices.

74
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,500
We would allocate 60 minutes for the meeting,

75
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,500
where 15 minutes could be reading time,

76
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:22,500
and the rest of the time was available for discussion.

77
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,500
OP1 meetings and documents are more complex.

78
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,000
We would still have the limit of six pages and appendices,

79
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,500
but the difference is that we would allow more time for the meeting itself

80
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:34,500
because the discussion would be longer,

81
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,500
hence the 90 to 120 minute timing.

82
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,500
We would also have time to leave slack time between meetings.

83
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,300
Not only can the meetings get spirited

84
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:45,300
and downtime is necessary in between,

85
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:49,500
but more importantly, new issues would surface during the OP1 review,

86
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:53,000
and it would take longer than expected to resolve those issues.

87
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
At Amazon, we favored letting the meeting run over its allocated time

88
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,600
because we had all the right people in the room,

89
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,200
and it was much easier to keep the meeting going

90
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,200
than to schedule a second meeting.

91
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:09,000
The OP1 plan is not going to be approved at the end of the meeting

92
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,300
because the company has a constrained set of resources

93
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:15,300
and the executive team needs to review all OP1 plans

94
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:19,000
before deciding how to allocate those scarce resources.
